{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — a non-canonical peer reference in a minted cap pattern, for a peer whose key the impl holds, is stored in canonical form.",
    "",
    "Batch 4. §3.6 cap-pattern canonicalization, rule 1. The section heads three \"normative implementation",
    "behaviors\" under one MUST; rules 1 and 3 each carry an act a peer can skip independently, so each is its own",
    "file (rule 3 is two). Rule 2 has no inbound observation (F32) and is not authored."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "A policy entry configured with a peers: reference in SHA-256 form, naming a peer whose public key the impl already holds, reads back with that reference in canonical form only",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §3.6 ('Peer references in cap patterns (`peers:` IdScope ...) MUST be canonical form per §1.5. Three normative implementation behaviors: 1. At mint with pubkey available — a non-canonical Base58 in `peers: include = [...]` is canonicalized before storage; the stored policy carries canonical form only.')",
  "spec_also": [
    "§1.5 carve-out ('Before any tree-lookup, cap-state operation, or stored-policy entry, the impl MUST canonicalize')",
    "§3.6 id-scope pattern grammar (peer patterns are flat literal ids or `*`) — see F32",
    "§3.6 prose after the operation types ('The `configure` operation accepts a `system/capability/policy-entry` and writes it at `system/capability/policy/{peer_pattern}`')",
    "§6.2 writing policy entries (configure is 'the recommended path for future audit/validation/normalization invariants; raw `tree:put` skips those')",
    "§6.2 ('MUST provide all four operations on the capability handler (`request`, `revoke`, `configure`, `delegate`)')",
    "§1.5 ('Cap patterns are form-bearing strings (`peers: include = [\"/{peer_id_form}/...\"]`)') — path-shaped, F32"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F32, F37",
  "reading": [
    "THE LEVEL. The rules are introduced as \"Three normative implementation behaviors\" directly after the MUST,",
    "and rule 1 is written in the indicative (\"is canonicalized before storage; the stored policy carries canonical",
    "form only\"). §1.5 states the same act with its own keyword: \"Before any ... stored-policy entry, the impl MUST",
    "canonicalize\". So rule 1 is keyworded twice, and `level_basis` is the default.",
    "",
    "WHAT \"MINT\" IS, AND WHERE \"THE STORED POLICY\" IS READ. Rule 1 says \"the stored policy carries canonical form",
    "only\", so the act is writing policy. The spec gives two ways: `system/capability:configure`, which (§3.6) \"accepts",
    "a `system/capability/policy-entry` and writes it at `system/capability/policy/{peer_pattern}`\", and a raw",
    "`system/tree:put` to the same path, which §6.2 says \"also works\" — and of which it says configure is \"the",
    "recommended path for future audit/validation/normalization invariants; raw `tree:put` skips those\". A raw put is",
    "a write the handler never sees, so rule 1 cannot bind it. ⚠ Corrected after review: a first draft said configure",
    "was the ONLY way. So \"mint\" is scoped here to `configure`, the only handler-mediated policy write, and the stored",
    "entry is read back with `system/tree:get`. (Elsewhere §6.2 uses \"mint\" for issuing a token via `request`; a token",
    "is not stored policy, so it is not rule 1's object.)",
    "",
    "\"PUBKEY AVAILABLE\". Rule 1: \"Available when the impl has prior contact with the named peer (pubkey recorded at",
    "handshake)\". The suite makes that contact first, as identity K, then configures a pattern naming S(K). The",
    "peer's OWN id is a second member: the impl always holds its own key, so \"pubkey available\" holds trivially.",
    "",
    "THE SHAPE OF THE REFERENCE (F32). §3.6's rule text writes the peer reference as `\"/{peer_id}/path\"`; the same",
    "section's id-scope grammar says \"peer-ids are flat, so peer patterns use a literal id or `*`\" and that a",
    "pattern \"carrying that path syntax (`/*/get`, `/{peer}/op`, `/*/*`) is therefore matched only as a literal",
    "string\". The grammar is the later statement by revision (0.8.1, F40) and the more specific one — though not the later",
    "in document order, and the path shape also appears in §1.5 and §6.2 — so the scored member uses the flat form.",
    "The path-shaped form is a witness-only member: what the peer does with it is the question F32 routes.",
    "",
    "`exclude` AS WELL AS `include`. Rule 1's example names `peers: include = [...]`. The exclude member rests on the",
    "two broader sentences — §3.6 \"Peer references in cap patterns (`peers:` IdScope ...) MUST be canonical form\" and",
    "§1.5 \"Before any ... stored-policy entry, the impl MUST canonicalize\" — neither of which is limited to include.",
    "It is scored, and flagged: it is the member most likely to be ruled out of scope, and the one whose failure",
    "matters most (F32's fail-open direction).",
    "",
    "WHAT IS NOT ASSERTED. That the read-back entity's content_hash equals the configured one — it cannot, if the",
    "MUST was honoured. That other fields are untouched — not stated; witnessed only."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "system/capability: configure",
      "system/tree: get on system/capability/policy/*"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller",
      "K (a second suite identity, handshake completed before the configure)"
    ],
    "handlers": [
      "system/capability with the configure operation"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy"
    ],
    "note": "§9.5's blurry-edge ruling makes the capability handler and its `configure` operation core ('its `delegate`/`revoke`/`configure` operations are core'), though §9.1 gives neither a row and §6.9's bootstrap list omits the handler (F33). §6.2 also: 'MUST provide all four operations on the capability handler'. A peer without configure is failing that, not this: here it is UNMEASURABLE, never passed. Posture-dependent: configure needs a grant the bootstrap posture may not give. A peer that refuses the configure outright under strict local policy is witnessed: §1.5's refusal permission names 'construction or handshake', not mint (F35)."
  },
  "domain": {
    "axis": "peer_reference_with_key_held",
    "members": [
      "peers.include = [S(K)], K contacted first",
      "peers.include = [S(self)]",
      "peers.exclude = [S(K)] with include [\"*\"] — the fail-open direction if left literal (F32)",
      "(witness only) peers.include = [\"/{S(K)}/cnf/*\"], the path-shaped form §3.6's rule text uses"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "canonical-at-rest",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "K has completed a handshake; the caller configures a policy entry whose single grant-entry carries the domain member",
      "why": "rule 1: 'the stored policy carries canonical form only'.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "contact_K",
          "identity": "K"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "configure",
          "uri": "system/capability",
          "operation": "configure",
          "note": "policy-entry {peer_pattern: <hex of a third suite identity>, grants: [{handlers: {include: [\"system/tree\"]}, resources: {include: [\"cnf/*\"]}, operations: {include: [\"get\"]}, peers: <member>}]}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_back",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/<that hex>"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "configure",
          "equals": 200
        },
        {
          "kind": "peers_scope_values_equal",
          "capture": "read_back",
          "equals": "the member with every S(x) replaced by C(x)",
          "why": "canonical form ONLY: an entry carrying both forms fails, because a stored S(x) is exactly what the rule forbids."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same configure, to a different peer_pattern (a fourth suite identity's hex), with the member already in canonical form — C(K), C(self)",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY. Proves configure → get round-trips the `peers` scope unchanged on this peer, so a mismatch in the main arm is attributable to canonicalization and not to a handler that drops, reorders or re-shapes `peers`. It also catches the over-eager reverse: a peer 'canonicalizing' an already-canonical id into the SHA-256 form.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "configure_canonical",
          "uri": "system/capability",
          "operation": "configure",
          "note": "policy-entry {peer_pattern: <hex of a fourth suite identity>, grants: [the same grant-entry, peers: <member with every S(x) replaced by C(x)>]}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_back_canonical",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/<that hex>"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "peers_scope_values_equal",
          "capture": "read_back_canonical",
          "equals": "the member, unchanged"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that stores policy verbatim",
      "expect": "read-back `peers` carries S(K)",
      "why": "rule 2 then string-compares a canonical runtime id against S(K) and never matches: an include silently grants nothing, and an exclude silently excludes nothing — the second is an over-grant."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "peer_canonicalization/peer_pattern_1_canonical_match",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only. The check's citation describes rule 2 (match), which this file does not author (F32); rule 1 is the part of §3.6 with an inbound observation."
  }
}
